WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.450 --> 00:00:10.550
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
again to 8.701.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:13.880
So this is the fifth
section of our introduction.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:17.510
I'd like to talk about the early
history and the people involved

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:19.640
in nuclear and particle physics.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:23.390
I cover the period from
1820 to the beginning

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:25.140
of the Second World War.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.830
other elements of the later
history of the development

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:29.450
of the standard model--

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:32.189
parity violation, CP violation--

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:33.890
those aspects will
be covered when

00:00:33.890 --> 00:00:36.500
we talk about the
actual physics involved.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:40.020
But I'd like to give you
some more background.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:42.380
Especially since we start
the discussion with particle

00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:45.830
physics, it's good
to understand what

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.590
was the starting ground,
which shoulders did

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:51.860
people stand on at the time.

00:00:51.860 --> 00:00:53.870
Important to realize
here, at this point,

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:55.670
I'm not an historian.

00:00:55.670 --> 00:00:57.650
I like to read about history.

00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:00.690
I just finished an
interesting book on Einstein.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:03.680
I like to have a good
understanding how

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.490
the people of the time, the
time itself, and the physics

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.320
discovery interacted.

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:16.633
It helps me in understanding
the process of being scientific.

00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:18.050
When you look at
history, you find

00:01:18.050 --> 00:01:20.420
a lot of places
where progress was

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:27.320
made by curiosity and by
doing things which are not

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.360
the common way to proceed.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.620
And so one learns this
by looking at history.

00:01:32.620 --> 00:01:36.850
And I might give you a number
of examples here as well.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.440
So diving in one of the
questions at the time,

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:48.760
going back, again, almost 200
years is how old was the Earth?

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:50.710
How old is the Earth?

00:01:50.710 --> 00:01:56.260
And about 200 years ago, people
started to argue whether or not

00:01:56.260 --> 00:01:59.410
the 10,000 of years,
which was long

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:04.030
thought to be the age of the
Earth, are actually correct.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:09.250
And specifically geologists
and biologists argued that this

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:10.419
cannot be true.

00:02:10.419 --> 00:02:15.640
They observed how slowly
geological and biological

00:02:15.640 --> 00:02:19.390
processes such as erosion
and evolution occur.

00:02:22.590 --> 00:02:29.410
And if you just try
to, by observation, put

00:02:29.410 --> 00:02:32.860
all of those ducks in
a row, if you want,

00:02:32.860 --> 00:02:35.650
you find that the Earth
must be much, much older

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:38.290
than those 10,000 years.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:44.950
On the contrary, some physicists
argued that the Earth cannot be

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.580
as old as several hundreds of
millions of years because it

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.690
would be, by now, a very
cold and dark place.

00:02:52.690 --> 00:02:58.330
One of the opposers of
evolution was Lord Kelvin,

00:02:58.330 --> 00:02:59.770
or William Thompson.

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:05.710
And he argued with classical
thermodynamics calculations

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:11.090
that the Earth cannot be as
old as those 300 million years

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:14.860
as Darwin writes in initial
printing of The Origin

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:17.480
of Species.

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.680
Herman Helmholtz,
a few years later,

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:27.570
tried to use energy compensation
principles to calculate

00:03:27.570 --> 00:03:34.040
how much heat from the sun would
radiate if the energy comes

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:35.930
from slow contraction.

00:03:35.930 --> 00:03:41.890
And he, by converting
gravitational potential energy

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:46.580
to heat, calculated age cannot
be more than 18 million years.

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:49.810
So putting those together,
you find, on the one side,

00:03:49.810 --> 00:03:53.770
the physicists,
theology might be

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.610
a different dimension
to this discussion,

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:58.060
and then geologists
and biologists.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:01.358
And a complicated question.

00:04:01.358 --> 00:04:03.400
I mean, really there was
something to be learned.

00:04:03.400 --> 00:04:05.668
Something was not
quite understood.

00:04:05.668 --> 00:04:07.210
And so we come back
to this question.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.700
Next slide.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:20.279
But then progress was made in
the understanding of physics.

00:04:20.279 --> 00:04:25.140
And here to be named are
Henri Becquerel, for example,

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:29.400
for the discovery of
radiation from uranium

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.040
and Ernest Rutherford
for the discovery,

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.680
by studying this
radiation, that there

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:38.400
must be at least two different
sources of radiation.

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.100
And he called them, simply by
following the Greek alphabet,

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.830
alpha and beta rays.

00:04:43.830 --> 00:04:46.080
In the same year, J.
J. Thompson discovered

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:50.010
a particle, the electron charged
particle, or the electron.

00:04:53.480 --> 00:04:55.490
Becquerel's story is
quite interesting,

00:04:55.490 --> 00:05:02.750
as he was trying to understand
the material Roentgen studied.

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:07.090
And he was interested
in figuring out what

00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:09.200
fluorescence material can do.

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.530
And again, this is one of the
examples where he, by accident,

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:16.280
discovered that it is
actually not the fact

00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:19.970
that you have a material,
you expose this to sunlight,

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:22.950
you wait a little while, and
it still radiates the light.

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:26.030
So this has delayed the
fluorescence of the material.

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:31.080
It is not quite the full story
to some fluorescent materials.

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:32.820
So he discovered
this by accident,

00:05:32.820 --> 00:05:35.210
by putting the mineral
in a drawer, together

00:05:35.210 --> 00:05:40.370
with a photo plate,
and found that there

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:47.450
was only a very short, limited
evidence from that photo plate

00:05:47.450 --> 00:05:49.370
to be radiated by the sun.

00:05:49.370 --> 00:05:52.610
But it was basically foggy
from being in the same drawer

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:53.600
as a mineral.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.380
But this was a rather
accidental discovery.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:06.450
Marie and Pierre Curie proposed
the new term, radioactivity,

00:06:06.450 --> 00:06:08.820
for materials which
generally emit light.

00:06:08.820 --> 00:06:11.700
And they discovered
additional materials

00:06:11.700 --> 00:06:17.670
to the uranium which was
discovered by Becquerel.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.730
So they discovered
thorium, for example,

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:22.215
and later also the elements
of polonium and radium.

00:06:22.215 --> 00:06:24.900
And they discovered
that those elements

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:28.050
radiate a lot of radioactivity.

00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.780
So Marie Curie was able to
measure the energy being

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:33.720
radiated, and found
that a gram of radium

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:38.840
can emit up to 140
calories per hour.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:42.840
And so you find that
a gram of radium

00:06:42.840 --> 00:06:45.510
is able to power, basically,
the energy you need in order

00:06:45.510 --> 00:06:47.070
to survive--

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:48.000
provide the energy.

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.840
So moving a little
forward, so then uranium

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:57.230
specifically, but other
radioactive materials,

00:06:57.230 --> 00:06:58.340
were studied.

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:02.620
And Paul Villard
discovered that there

00:07:02.620 --> 00:07:05.230
must be a third component
of radiation which behaves

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:07.000
different from the other two.

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:09.610
And he called these
gamma rays, again,

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:14.140
simply following the
alphabet and moving along

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:17.110
to the third letter.

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:21.820
Rutherford then connects
these findings first

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:24.670
to the question of
the age of the Earth.

00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:28.960
And he simply suggests that
it's those radioactive elements

00:07:28.960 --> 00:07:33.070
which are in ores in the core
of the Earth which provide

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:36.400
additional source
of feed sufficient,

00:07:36.400 --> 00:07:39.790
because of the
connectivity of the Earth,

00:07:39.790 --> 00:07:43.570
to keep the Earth
geologically active.

00:07:43.570 --> 00:07:45.820
And he comes to the
conclusion that the Earth

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:48.310
might be as well a
few billion years old,

00:07:48.310 --> 00:07:51.730
as we know now it is.

00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:55.000
So putting this in context,
at the very same time

00:07:55.000 --> 00:08:02.320
in Bern, Switzerland, a
clerk named Albert Einstein

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.060
has a fantastic year.

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:07.420
He, in one year, comes
up with a sequence

00:08:07.420 --> 00:08:09.220
of theoretical discoveries.

00:08:09.220 --> 00:08:11.710
One is special relativity.

00:08:11.710 --> 00:08:14.770
And he uses the findings
of special relativity

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:17.560
to derive that there's an
equivalence between energy

00:08:17.560 --> 00:08:18.580
and mass.

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:20.530
And this equivalence,
as we will see later,

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:23.080
when we discussed nuclear
physics specifically,

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:28.480
is very important to understand
nuclear decay, nuclear fission,

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:33.070
and nuclear fusion,
and figuring out why,

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:35.320
if you have a
component state that

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:38.559
seems to be lighter than the
sum of the individual components

00:08:38.559 --> 00:08:40.830
making up this particle.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:45.610
Rutherford was a pioneer
with collider experiments

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:48.880
in the sense that he used
other particles a lot

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.790
to bombard all
kinds of materials.

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:57.520
So what he found first is
that, if alpha particles,

00:08:57.520 --> 00:08:59.780
when stopped, turn into helium.

00:08:59.780 --> 00:09:01.600
So the alpha
particle itself grabs

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.840
on to the electrons from
the material it collides in.

00:09:09.570 --> 00:09:11.003
There was a technical problem.

00:09:11.003 --> 00:09:11.795
I'll just continue.

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:17.020
And that turns into helium.

00:09:20.830 --> 00:09:22.420
His students,
Marsden and Geiger,

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:25.180
then perform a very famous
gold foil experiment.

00:09:25.180 --> 00:09:27.580
So you all probably
have heard about this,

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:30.940
taking an alpha particle
source and you shine it

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:35.080
on a foil of gold.

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:38.350
And then you look at the angular
distribution of the particles,

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:40.565
which go through
or which have been

00:09:40.565 --> 00:09:42.490
backscattered from this foil.

00:09:42.490 --> 00:09:45.580
And Rutherford then
takes those measurements

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:49.270
and turns them into a
solar-system-like model

00:09:49.270 --> 00:09:52.150
of atoms which are
essentially made out

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:56.630
of empty space and a very
small, intense nuclei.

00:09:56.630 --> 00:09:59.410
So Rutherford then continues
with this experiment

00:09:59.410 --> 00:10:06.130
and it's able to produce,
by bombarding nitrogen

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:08.990
with other particles,
protons and oxygen.

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:12.070
And this is, in fact, the
first human-engineered nuclear

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:12.740
reaction.

00:10:12.740 --> 00:10:17.050
So now we are in the year 1919,
just after the First World

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.390
War ended.

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:21.250
On the theoretical
side, this is the time

00:10:21.250 --> 00:10:24.490
that quantum mechanics
is developed.

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:26.530
And Dirac then
combines relativity

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:30.010
with quantum
mechanics, which then

00:10:30.010 --> 00:10:32.020
leads to the so-called
Dirac equation, which

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:37.540
we're going to look at very
shortly in this class as well.

00:10:37.540 --> 00:10:39.160
This equation is
quite interesting

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:41.740
because it predicts the
existence of negative energy

00:10:41.740 --> 00:10:43.010
states.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:46.180
And so then that just
comes out of the equations.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:49.100
And then you ask yourself,
what's happening here?

00:10:49.100 --> 00:10:52.990
You can have an interpretation
that, for an electron,

00:10:52.990 --> 00:10:55.660
of the electron which
traveled backwards in time,

00:10:55.660 --> 00:10:59.380
or you interpret
them as electrons

00:10:59.380 --> 00:11:01.100
with negative energies.

00:11:01.100 --> 00:11:04.600
And so this then leads to
the prediction of antimatter.

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:14.020
Pauli and Fermi, they're
puzzled by a problem

00:11:14.020 --> 00:11:17.720
of energy conservation
in the second case.

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:22.810
And so this is something
which is rather weird

00:11:22.810 --> 00:11:25.630
and is a big challenge to
the physics of the time.

00:11:25.630 --> 00:11:27.310
And they've solved
this challenge

00:11:27.310 --> 00:11:30.790
by proposing a new particle
which is rather light

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:32.650
and doesn't interact
with the detectors they

00:11:32.650 --> 00:11:33.890
had available at the time.

00:11:33.890 --> 00:11:36.520
So it just escapes undetected.

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:38.890
They call this
particle the neutrino.

00:11:38.890 --> 00:11:41.410
A year later,
neutrons are directly

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:44.260
detected in
experiments by Chadwick

00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:47.110
with beryllium and
other particles again.

00:11:47.110 --> 00:11:51.100
And then the predicted
anti-electrons, the positrons,

00:11:51.100 --> 00:11:55.840
were discovered by Anderson in
tracts of photographic plates

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:57.340
which looked like
electrons but they

00:11:57.340 --> 00:11:58.880
curve in the wrong direction.

00:11:58.880 --> 00:12:01.420
So either they have
the opposite charge

00:12:01.420 --> 00:12:03.410
or they travel
backwards in time.

00:12:03.410 --> 00:12:06.356
They didn't have quite the
time resolution to [INAUDIBLE]..

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:15.020
All right, also on
the theoretical side,

00:12:15.020 --> 00:12:19.040
it needed to be understood how
neutrons and protons actually

00:12:19.040 --> 00:12:21.840
bind together in nuclei.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:28.580
And so Hideki Yukawa proposes
the existence of a strong force

00:12:28.580 --> 00:12:31.100
which is really, really
strong and binds those nuclear

00:12:31.100 --> 00:12:35.570
together to a degree that you
cannot easily break them apart.

00:12:35.570 --> 00:12:40.460
And then Bethe calculates how
nuclear fusion, rather than

00:12:40.460 --> 00:12:44.180
the fission process, can be
used in order to power the sun.

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:48.500
So for this, he proposes
a three-step process,

00:12:48.500 --> 00:12:50.610
the so-called
proton-proton chain,

00:12:50.610 --> 00:12:52.820
which I will not discuss
here but we will certainly

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.040
discuss later in this class.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:57.620
And then there's
more developments

00:12:57.620 --> 00:13:03.470
in the area of nuclear physics.

00:13:03.470 --> 00:13:06.780
And this progress
is made by, again,

00:13:06.780 --> 00:13:11.462
using all kinds of materials
and bombarding with each other.

00:13:11.462 --> 00:13:17.660
So for example, by colliding
neutrons with uranium,

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:20.150
one discovers a process
of nuclear fission.

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:23.750
This was done by Lise
Meitner and Otto Hahn

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:27.980
in the late 1930s.

00:13:27.980 --> 00:13:33.790
So from there on, there's
interesting further

00:13:33.790 --> 00:13:36.010
developments going
on in the sense

00:13:36.010 --> 00:13:44.075
that many physicists
at the time in Europe

00:13:44.075 --> 00:13:45.880
are rather concerned
by the developments

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:48.760
of the Nazi Party in Germany.

00:13:48.760 --> 00:13:52.690
In the '40s already after the
start of the Second World War,

00:13:52.690 --> 00:13:56.980
Albert Einstein wrote a letter
to Theodore Roosevelt pointing

00:13:56.980 --> 00:14:00.740
out that there is a real
threat that the Nazis are

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:06.870
going to develop a bomb
based on nuclear processes.

00:14:06.870 --> 00:14:09.620
And so this then led to
the Manhattan Program

00:14:09.620 --> 00:14:13.280
in the US and the development of
the first nuclear bombs or atom

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:13.820
bombs.

00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:18.530
And in August 1945,
the first two bombs

00:14:18.530 --> 00:14:22.070
were dropped on
Japan, which led then

00:14:22.070 --> 00:14:24.830
to the surrender of
the Japanese empire

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:27.860
and the end of the
Second World War.

00:14:27.860 --> 00:14:32.690
With that, I stop the discussion
of those early developments.

00:14:32.690 --> 00:14:35.330
I hope you got a
first glimpse and use

00:14:35.330 --> 00:14:38.960
this as a starting
point to read further.

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.090
Those characters, Lise
Meitner, for example--

00:14:41.090 --> 00:14:43.820
I'm looking at her
picture right now--

00:14:43.820 --> 00:14:47.930
very, very interesting to
see how those people were

00:14:47.930 --> 00:14:51.710
connected, how those
people communicated,

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:54.020
and in which environment
they had to work.

00:14:54.020 --> 00:14:56.750
Lise Meitner, for
example, was Jewish.

00:14:56.750 --> 00:15:01.220
And she left Europe, had to
flee from the Nazis in the '30s,

00:15:01.220 --> 00:15:05.090
while making these
kind of discoveries.

00:15:05.090 --> 00:15:07.880
Also interesting is maybe
the historical introduction

00:15:07.880 --> 00:15:09.290
to elementary particles.

00:15:09.290 --> 00:15:13.880
I have this here in
David Griffiths' book.

00:15:13.880 --> 00:15:16.700
This starts with this
kind of classical era

00:15:16.700 --> 00:15:19.220
and then goes beyond
the Second World War

00:15:19.220 --> 00:15:24.270
and introduces the findings in
particle physics beyond what I

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:25.910
explained to this point.

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:29.020
So I hope you enjoyed this.

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:32.090
This is basically the last of
these introductory lectures

00:15:32.090 --> 00:15:34.610
which doesn't come
with a set of problems,

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:37.740
with a set of things you
should be interacting with.

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:40.673
So the next one will
already do that.

00:15:40.673 --> 00:15:44.990
And we will use
this in the Thursday

00:15:44.990 --> 00:15:52.600
recitation of the first
week to have discussion.

